[?2004l[?2004h> # Post-Compromise Intrusion Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Executive Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> On 2026-08-11, host `smb-prod-01` was compromised by an external threat actor operating from IP `198.51.100.218` (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RegBack/SECURITY.bak`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`). The primary entry vector was remote exploitation of unpatched SMBv1 (MS17-010 / CVE-2017-0144) (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`, `/sas/vulns.json:3`). Following initial access, the adversary executed PowerShell, dumped LSASS credentials, deployed ransomware, set up stealthy persistence via WMI event subscriptions and DLL search-order hijacking, and cleared System event logs to obscure evidence (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:8`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Attack Chain & ATT&CK Mapping
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> | Step | Technique ID | Technique Name | Alert Status | Evidence / Citation |
[?2004l[?2004h> |---|---|---|---|---|
[?2004l[?2004h> | 1 | T1210 | Exploitation of Remote Services | Alerted (`ALERT-01`) | `/disk/Windows/Temp/mssecsvc.exe`, `/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`, `/sas/alerts.json:2` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 2 | T1059.001 | PowerShell | Alerted | `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:10` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 3 | T1003.001 | LSASS Memory Credential Dump | Alerted (`ALERT-02`) | `/disk/Windows/Temp/lsass.dmp`, `/sas/alerts.json:10` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 4 | T1486 | Data Encrypted for Impact | Alerted (`ALERT-03`) | `/disk/Users/Public/enc.exe`, `/sas/alerts.json:18` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 5 | T1546.003 | WMI Event Subscription Persistence | Silent (Un-alerted) | `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 6 | T1574.001 | DLL Search-Order Hijacking | Silent (Un-alerted) | `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll` |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 7 | T1070.001 | Clear Windows Event Logs | Silent (Un-alerted) | `/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx` |
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Observed Entities & Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Attacker Source IP**: `198.51.100.218` (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RegBack/SECURITY.bak`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **C2 Domain**: `update-smb.example.cn` (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RegBack/SECURITY.bak`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:22`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Mining Pool Address**: `stratum+tcp://xmr.smb.evil:3333` (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA`, `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll`, `/disk/Users/Public/enc.exe`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:23`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Crypto Wallet**: `4A000000000000000000c814c46d6253` (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA`, `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll`, `/disk/Users/Public/enc.exe`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:24`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Compromised Target Host**: `smb-prod-01` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 4. Persistence Details
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **WMI Event Subscription (T1546.003)**: An `__EventFilter` bound to a `CommandLineEventConsumer` was installed in the WMI repository (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA`) to launch background coinmining/C2 operations autonomously.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **DLL Search-Order Hijacking (T1574.001)**: A malicious DLL was dropped as `wbemcomn.dll` in `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll` to sideload into legitimate WMI process executions.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 5. Anti-Forensics & Impact Analysis
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Anti-Forensics**: The attacker cleared the Windows System event log (`EventID 1102`) to wipe execution traces (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Credential dumping occurred via LSASS memory extraction (`/disk/Windows/Temp/lsass.dmp`), followed by ransomware execution (`/disk/Users/Public/enc.exe`) targeting user files.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@64d515f1415e:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Vulnerability Report: Entry Vector Analysis
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Primary Entry Vector Overview
[?2004l[?2004h> The host `smb-prod-01` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`) was compromised via remote exploitation of an unpatched SMBv1 service (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`, `/sas/baseline.json:2`). The vulnerability leveraged by the attacker was **CVE-2017-0144** (MS17-010 / EternalBlue) (`/sas/vulns.json:3`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:16`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Vulnerability Details
[?2004l[?2004h> - **CVE Identifier**: CVE-2017-0144 (`/sas/vulns.json:3`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:16`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Severity Rating**: CVSS v3.1 Base Score 8.8 (HIGH) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:16`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Vulnerability Class**: Remote Code Execution / Buffer Overflow in SMBv1 Server Driver
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Vulnerable Component**: `/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys` (`/sas/alerts.json:26`, `/sas/baseline.json:2`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Exploit Implant**: `/disk/Windows/Temp/mssecsvc.exe` (`/sas/alerts.json:2`, `/sas/vulns.json:3`)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 3. Technical Mechanism & Exploit Artifacts
[?2004l[?2004h> The vulnerability resides in Microsoft Windows SMBv1 protocol processing within `srv.sys` (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`). Remote unauthenticated attackers send specially crafted SMBv1 packets to port 445, triggering memory corruption in kernel space to execute arbitrary code. Upon successful exploitation, the DoublePulsar payload was injected, which dropped and executed the SMB exploit implant `mssecsvc.exe` located at `/disk/Windows/Temp/mssecsvc.exe` (`/sas/alerts.json:2`, `/sas/vulns.json:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 4. Alternate Entry Vector Analysis
[?2004l[?2004h> All other potential entry vectors (such as RDP / BlueKeep CVE-2019-0708 mentioned erroneously in initial tickets) were evaluated and ruled out based on disk and network evidence (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:8`, `/disk/inetpub/logs/LogFiles/W3SVC1/u_ex.log`). Web server logs (`/disk/inetpub/logs/LogFiles/W3SVC1/u_ex.log`) showed standard HTTP GET traffic without web exploitation attempts. The sole successful initial access vector was CVE-2017-0144 via SMBv1 (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`, `/sas/vulns.json:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@64d515f1415e:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Baseline & Security Configuration Assessment
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Executive Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> The forensic evaluation of `smb-prod-01` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`) revealed multiple baseline security configuration deficiencies and missing hardening controls. These weaknesses enabled initial exploitation via legacy SMBv1, credential dumping, stealth persistence, and unmonitored event log clearing (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`, `/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`, `/sas/baseline.json:2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 2. Identified Baseline & Configuration Deficiencies
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 1. Enablement of Legacy SMBv1 Protocol (BAS-01)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Check ID**: CHK-07 / BAS-01 (`/sas/baseline.json:2`, `/sas/alerts.json:26`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Finding**: SMBv1 protocol driver `srv.sys` is active and missing security patches (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`, `/sas/baseline.json:2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk**: Legacy SMBv1 lacks modern security controls and is vulnerable to remote unauthenticated buffer overflows (CVE-2017-0144 / MS17-010).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Baseline Fix**: Disable SMBv1 protocol globally via PowerShell/Group Policy (`Disable-WindowsOptionalFeature -Online -FeatureName SMB1Protocol`) and ensure MS17-010 security update is applied (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 2. Lack of WMI Event Subscription Auditing & Integrity Guardrails
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Finding**: Threat actor registered a malicious `__EventFilter` and `CommandLineEventConsumer` in the WMI repository (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk**: Unmonitored WMI event subscriptions provide persistent execution under SYSTEM privileges without creating traditional startup entries or service alerts.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Baseline Fix**: Enable WMI activity auditing (Event ID 5861 in WMI-Activity log), restrict WMI namespace permissions, and implement baseline monitoring for repository modifications (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 3. Insecure Application Search Path / Sideloading Weakness
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Finding**: Attacker placed a hijacked DLL `wbemcomn.dll` in `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll`.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk**: Search-order hijacking allows malicious code execution whenever WMI or system services attempt to load missing or higher-priority DLLs.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Baseline Fix**: Restrict directory write permissions, enable Safe DLL Search Mode, and enforce AppLocker/WDAC code signing policies (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 4. Insufficient Event Log Auditing & Protection Controls
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Finding**: Attacker cleared System event log (`EventID 1102`) silently without triggering security alerts (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk**: Log clearing destroys forensic artifacts and hides ongoing post-exploitation activities.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Baseline Fix**: Configure central SIEM event forwarding, restrict Event Log clearing permissions, and alert on Event ID 1102 / Event ID 104 (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 5. LSASS Memory Protection Deficiency
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Finding**: Attacker dumped LSASS process memory into `/disk/Windows/Temp/lsass.dmp` (`/sas/alerts.json:10`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk**: Unprotected LSASS allows plaintext credential and ticket extraction.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Baseline Fix**: Enable LSA Protection (RunAsPPL) in registry and enable Credential Guard (`/disk/Windows/Temp/lsass.dmp`).
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@64d515f1415e:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/remediation-plan.md
[?2004lem32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll`).

### Phase 3: [?2004h> # Prioritised Remediation & Action Plan
[?2004lin[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## 1. Reconciled Change Ticket (CHG-1677)
[?2004l[?2004h> This section reconciles Change Ticket `CHG-1677` (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:1`) against evidence gathered from `/disk` and `/sas`. In accordance with DFIR standards, backup and quarantine actions are preferred over outright deletion.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
